-- [Chemistry of the Groups] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'All four hydrogen halides (HF, HCl, HBr, HI) dissolve in water to give acidic solutions, yet their measured acid strengths differ markedly: HCl, HBr, and HI are all strong acids, whereas hydrofluoric acid is only weakly ionized. Which statement best explains why HF is the weakest acid of the four despite fluorine being the most electronegative halogen?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The exceptionally strong H–F bond resists ionization, so relatively little HF dissociates to release \\(\\text{H}^+\\) in water."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fluorine''s high electronegativity pulls the shared pair so strongly that the H–F bond ionizes more readily than any other hydrogen halide."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Because fluorine is the smallest halogen, the compact \\(\\text{F}^-\\) ion is a very stable conjugate base that drives ionization nearly to completion."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fluorine''s single unpaired valence electron leaves HF with an incomplete octet, preventing it from donating a proton to water."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' For the binary hydrogen halides of group 17, acid strength in water increases down the group, HF < HCl < HBr < HI, because the H–A bond strength decreases as the halogen gets larger; the weaker the H–A bond, the more readily the molecule ionizes to release \(\text{H}^+\). Fluorine is the smallest halogen and forms an unusually short, strong H–F bond, so HF alone is a weak acid while the others are strong acids. This trend is governed by bond strength, not by electronegativity, which is the key distinction being tested. (Choice A) is correct: the strong H–F bond is difficult to break, so only a small fraction of HF molecules ionize in water, making it a weak acid. (Choice B) is incorrect: it treats electronegativity as the controlling factor for the down-group trend, but greater bond polarity does not make the H–F bond easier to break; the strong H–F bond actually resists ionization, which is why HF is the weakest, not the strongest, acid. (Choice C) is incorrect: it invokes a real property (fluorine is the smallest halogen) but reaches the wrong conclusion; the small, highly charge-dense \(\text{F}^-\) ion is strongly hydrated and paired with the strong H–F bond, and the net result is weak, not enhanced, ionization. (Choice D) is incorrect: fluorine has seven valence electrons and completes its octet by sharing one pair with hydrogen, so HF is a normal closed-shell molecule with no incomplete octet; its weak acidity comes from bond strength, not an electronic deficiency. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the group 17 trend that binary hydrogen-halide acid strength is governed by decreasing H–A bond strength down the group, rather than by electronegativity, to explain the anomalous weakness of HF.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f4f7b834-bb1f-401a-b380-2f173274871c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f4f7b834-bb1f-401a-b380-2f173274871c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f4f7b834-bb1f-401a-b380-2f173274871c', 'B', 'misconception', 'acidity_tracks_electronegativity'),
  ('f4f7b834-bb1f-401a-b380-2f173274871c', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'true_property_wrong_conclusion'),
  ('f4f7b834-bb1f-401a-b380-2f173274871c', 'D', 'misconception', 'phantom_octet_deficiency');

-- [Periodic Table Structure & Classification] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In its binary compounds with reactive metals, a certain main-group nonmetal is consistently found as a monatomic ion carrying a charge of 2−, which it achieves by completing the octet of its outermost occupied shell. To which chemical family does this element belong?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The alkaline earth metals (Group 2)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The halogens (Group 17)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The chalcogens (Group 16)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The pnictogens (Group 15)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' The characteristic ionic charge of a main-group element is set by how many electrons its neutral atom must gain or lose to reach the nearest noble-gas configuration, and that count is fixed by the number of valence electrons in its group. An element that reaches an octet by gaining exactly two electrons (forming a 2− ion) must start two electrons short of eight, meaning six valence electrons. (Choice C) is correct: six valence electrons corresponds to the \(ns^2\,np^4\) configuration of Group 16, the chalcogens (oxygen, sulfur, selenium); gaining two electrons fills the p subshell to \(np^6\) and produces the observed 2− ion, as in oxide (\(\text{O}^{2-}\)) and sulfide (\(\text{S}^{2-}\)). (Choice A) is incorrect: the alkaline earth metals have two valence electrons (\(ns^2\)) and are metals that lose those two electrons to form 2+ cations, not 2− anions, so the sign of the charge is wrong even though the magnitude matches. (Choice B) is incorrect: the halogens have seven valence electrons (\(ns^2\,np^5\)) and complete their octet by gaining only one electron, giving a characteristic 1− ion rather than 2−. (Choice D) is incorrect: the pnictogens have five valence electrons (\(ns^2\,np^3\)) and must gain three electrons to reach an octet, so their characteristic monatomic anion is 3−, as in nitride (\(\text{N}^{3-}\)). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between valence electron count, octet completion, and characteristic ionic charge to infer the family from an observed chemical behavior.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a93c27e1-a7ec-4367-b169-e7d0347d5e3e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a93c27e1-a7ec-4367-b169-e7d0347d5e3e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a93c27e1-a7ec-4367-b169-e7d0347d5e3e', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'confusing gain of electrons (anion) with loss (cation) while matching charge magnitude'),
  ('a93c27e1-a7ec-4367-b169-e7d0347d5e3e', 'B', 'misconception', 'assuming any nonmetal anion forms a 2- ion / miscounting halogen valence electrons'),
  ('a93c27e1-a7ec-4367-b169-e7d0347d5e3e', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'selecting the neighboring group whose characteristic anion is 3-');

-- [Periodic Trends] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Across the second period, first ionization energy (\(\text{IE}_1\)) generally rises as nuclear charge increases. Yet boron (\(\text{IE}_1 = 801\ \text{kJ/mol}\)) is easier to ionize than beryllium (\(\text{IE}_1 = 899\ \text{kJ/mol}\)), even though boron has one more proton. What best accounts for boron''s lower first ionization energy?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The electron removed from boron comes from a 2p subshell, which lies higher in energy and is less tightly held than the filled 2s subshell that ionizes in beryllium"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ionizing boron produces a stable half-filled 2p subshell, and the extra stability of that product makes the electron easier to remove"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Boron''s larger atomic radius places its outermost electron farther from the nucleus, so the weaker attraction lowers the energy needed to remove it"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The added proton in boron increases electron-electron repulsion more than nuclear attraction, so the valence electron is pushed out more easily"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Variations of Chemical Properties with Group and Row.'' First ionization energy tends to increase across a period as nuclear charge grows, but small deviations occur each time electrons begin filling a new subshell, because within a shell the s electrons are lower in energy (more penetrating, less shielded) than the p electrons. Beryllium is \([\text{He}]2s^2\) and boron is \([\text{He}]2s^2 2p^1\), so the outermost electron in each element sits in a different subshell. (Choice A) is correct: the electron removed from boron is its single \(2p^1\) electron, which is higher in energy and less tightly bound than the \(2s\) electron removed from beryllium''s filled \(2s^2\) subshell; this energy difference outweighs boron''s slightly greater nuclear charge, producing the lower \(\text{IE}_1\). (Choice B) is incorrect: the half-filled-subshell stabilization argument explains the nitrogen-versus-oxygen dip, not the beryllium-versus-boron dip. Removing boron''s one \(2p\) electron leaves an empty \(2p\) subshell, not a half-filled one, so this reasoning is misapplied here. (Choice C) is incorrect: atomic radius actually decreases from beryllium to boron because the added proton raises the effective nuclear charge, so a larger-radius argument points the wrong way and cannot explain the lower ionization energy. (Choice D) is incorrect: the added proton increases nuclear attraction and generally raises ionization energy across a period; electron-electron repulsion between two paired electrons does not overwhelm that attraction here, and this misconception ignores the subshell-energy difference that is actually responsible. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply electron-configuration and subshell-energy reasoning to explain an anomaly in the periodic trend rather than recall the trend itself.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e';
